The geology of the area is dominated by domed granite outcrops. The vegetation in the park is mostly open eucalypt forest. Species such as the hoop pine, weeping bottlebrush, river she-oak and swamp mahogany can be found along creek banks. The main watercourse in the park is Crows Nest Creek, a tributary of Cressbrook Creek, itself a tributary of the Brisbane River.  About 4% of the park is riverine wetlands.
Crows Nest National Park is known for its rugged landscape, waterfalls, and a sparkling gorge called the Valley of Diamonds. This valley is so named because of the mineral, feldspar, which glistens in the sunlight. Popular activities include visiting the lookouts and observing the heath wildflowers, wildlife and birdwatching...","coordinates":[{"lat":-27.25388889,"lon":152.07416667,"primary":true,"globe":"earth"}],"pageimage":"Acacia_podalyriifolia_5.jpg","pageprops":{"page_image_free":"Acacia_podalyriifolia_5.jpg","wikibase-shortdesc":"National park in Queensland, Australia","wikibase_item":"Q1140798"}}]}}
